{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["And the sense of <b>May HaShem bless you and guard you</b> — by way of truth, the blessing is from above, and the guarding is that He guard it, in the manner of \"Remember and Guard\" (זכור ושמור). And further: <b>May HaShem</b> the Great <b>make His face shine</b> — those faces that gaze upon you. And thus they said in the Sifrei (Sifrei on Numbers 6:25): Rabbi Natan says: This is the light of the Shekhinah. <b>And be gracious to you</b> — that you find favor in His eyes, as they said (Genesis Rabbah 9:4): \"My world, My world, would that your quality of grace be before Me at every hour.\" And further: <b>May HaShem lift up His face</b> toward the heavens from above, <b>and grant you peace</b> — everything in your house. And thus they said in the Sifrei (Sifrei on Numbers 6:26): Rabbi Natan says: This is the peace of the kingdom of the House of David. <b>And they shall place My</b> great <b>name upon the children of Israel, and I will bless them</b> — for I am the speaker; here I am, and peace."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "Ramban here quotes the Sifrei attributing to Rabbi Natan the interpretation that the light in the priestly blessing is the light of the Shekhinah and that the peace is that of the Davidic kingdom; these are rendered exactly as they appear in the Hebrew source. The closing phrase 'כי אני המדבר הנני ושלום' is translated hyper-literally as 'for I am the speaker; here I am, and peace.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:03:11.885787609Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}